The package ggml provides CMake integration:

  find_package(ggml CONFIG REQUIRED)
  target_link_libraries(main PRIVATE ggml::ggml)

Windows builds enable LTO. Libraries built with clang-cl require a compatible
LLVM toolchain and lld-link in the consuming application.

Available vcpkg features:
  metal  - Metal GPU backend (macOS/iOS, auto-enabled on Apple)
  vulkan - Vulkan GPU backend
  cuda   - CUDA GPU backend
  opencl - OpenCL GPU backend

CUDA is server-only: Linux x64/ARM64 and Windows x64. Jetson CUDA 12.6
is outside this feature's scope. Linux targets match qvac-fabric's packages:
  Linux x64:   75-virtual;80-real;86-real;89-real;90-virtual;120a-real
  Linux ARM64: 121a-real

The virtual targets contain PTX for driver JIT compilation; real targets
contain native GPU code. The x64 module includes T4 PTX and native SM80
code. ARM64 ships only DGX Spark code to keep the npm platform package
within its size limit. This feature does not build a separate Jetson module.

Release builds use CUDA 13.0.3 (nvcc 13.0.88). Any non-empty
QVAC_CUDA_TOOLKIT marker requires that nvcc version; the marker text is an
ABI hash input, not a toolkit-name allowlist. Unmarked source builds with
another nvcc version warn and continue; unsupported targets fail in nvcc.
CUDACXX selects nvcc explicitly. Otherwise the search matches qvac-fabric:
CUDA_PATH hints, then the default search (including PATH), then /usr/local/cuda.
A resolved nvcc symlink selects CUDAToolkit_ROOT only when runtime headers
identify a toolkit prefix there; wrappers without a toolkit layout leave
normal CMake discovery in place.
Consumer triplets must hash QVAC_CUDA_TOOLKIT for PORT STREQUAL "ggml"
with VCPKG_ENV_PASSTHROUGH. Keep CUDACXX and CUDA_PATH via
VCPKG_KEEP_ENV_VARS, as setup-cuda does.

CUDA builds explicitly enable FlashAttention and its four default type
combinations. CUDA graphs remain off: this fork implements them for
llama.cpp, not diffusion. Windows CUDA source builds ship runtime DLLs;
the port checks for the platform's CUDA module on Linux and Windows.

On Linux the CUDA backend is a runtime-loaded module. Enable vulkan as well
to retain GPU fallback when CUDA cannot load or has no compatible device.
Automatic selection can then use Vulkan, or CPU if no GPU is available.
